Neural Networks (Moving Parts): license and attribution
=======================================================

This project contains several kinds of material under different terms,
because only some of it is adapted from a NonCommercial source. In short:

    Code                       MIT
      the application (src/, except the two exclusions below), the brand
      layer, and tools/. Full text in LICENSE-MIT.

    Course content             CC BY-NC 3.0
      src/modules/ and the exercise prompts and hints in
      src/exercises/*/index.ts, following their source.

    Reference implementations  MIT, Michael Nielsen's copyright retained
      src/python/reference_network.py, two functions in
      src/python/course_helpers.py, and the exercise solution.py files.

    The series name and mark   not licensed. See below.

The reason for the split is that the course content is adapted from a
NonCommercial source and carries that restriction, while the application that
runs it and the brand layer are original work containing none of Nielsen's
material, so nothing reaches them.

Course content (prose, pedagogical sequence, visualizations)
------------------------------------------------------------

Adapted from Michael A. Nielsen, "Neural Networks and Deep Learning",
Determination Press, 2015, licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial 3.0 Unported License (CC BY-NC 3.0):
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0/

The course content, meaning the module prose, the pedagogical sequence and
the interactive figures under src/modules/, is offered under CC BY-NC 3.0 to
match its source: share and adapt it with attribution, for non-commercial
purposes only.

Note that CC BY-NC 3.0 has no ShareAlike clause, so this is a choice to match
the source rather than an obligation to. What does bind is narrower: no use of
Nielsen's material for commercial advantage, and no granting of rights in his
material beyond those held.

The software: the application, the brand layer, tools
-----------------------------------------------------

Copyright (c) 2026 Pavol Chvala, and licensed under the MIT License, whose
full text is in LICENSE-MIT beside this file.

This covers everything under src/ except two things: the module prose in
src/modules/, and the exercise prompts and hints in src/exercises/*/index.ts,
both of which are course content under CC BY-NC 3.0 above. So the MIT grant
reaches the Pyodide worker and its protocol, the exercise harness and its UI,
the editor, the progress layer, the start page, the stylesheet, the app shell,
and each exercise's skeleton.py and tests.py, which are original work rather
than adapted from anything. It also covers the series brand layer in
src/brand/ and everything in tools/.

The exercise solution.py files are the exception inside the exception: they
are MIT as well, but adapted from Nielsen, so his notice travels with them.
See "Reference code" below.

None of the MIT-covered files contain Nielsen's prose or his code, so the
CC BY-NC grant does not reach them. Take the harness, take the worker, take
the brand layer, and build something else with them.

The two files with a foot in both camps are named explicitly under "Reference
code" below: they are MIT as well, and Nielsen's copyright notice travels with
them.


The method kit
--------------

The kit (the method, the casebook, the design-doc template, the brand files and
the slash commands) was extracted from this repository and now lives in the
series repository, at PavolC/moving-parts under course-kit/. Its licence travels
inside it, at course-kit/LICENSE there: the documents CC BY 4.0, the brand files
MIT. Nothing in this file grants anything over it, because none of it is here
any more.


The series name and mark
------------------------

Not licensed by any grant in this file: the name "Moving Parts", the names of
courses published under it, the three-band Moving Parts mark, and the sigmoid
glyph used as this course's own mark.

The MIT grant covers the code that draws those marks. It does not grant them.
Copy the brand layer, change the accent and the glyph in brand.ts, replace the
three bands in Monogram.tsx, and publish under your own name; that is what the
layer is built for.


Reference code
--------------

Some reference implementations are adapted from Michael Nielsen's code, and
they remain MIT with his copyright notice attached. They are:
src/python/reference_network.py; the backprop and init_network functions in
src/python/course_helpers.py; and the solution.py file of every exercise
except prepare. Each says so in its own header. The originals are at
https://github.com/mnielsen/neural-networks-and-deep-learning, which is
distributed under the MIT license:

